  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs high pressure departs off the Mid-Atlantic Coast today, an \nupper level trough will begin to dig south across the Great \nLakes region. This trough will support a deepening low centered \nover Ontario and will aid in the development of upstream \nconvection in the region beginning today. Remnants of this \nconvection are expected to move into the far northwestern \ncounties in the CWA late tonight, where SPC currently has a Day \n1 Marginal Risk (1 of 5). There remains uncertainty with how \norganized this convection will remain given the lack of \ninstability during a non- diurnally favorable time, but some \ngusty winds up to 60 mph will be possible. By the time they move\neast of the I-75 corridor, expecting them to be primary rain \nshowers and sub-severe, but cannot rule out a few isolated \nrumbles of thunder. \n\nOn Tuesday, the associated cold front is expected to push east \nacross the area, acting as a focal point for additional convection \nto develop. Confidence is high that there will be storms given the \nsynoptic support that this trough will provide, the tricky part is \nthe extent and magnitude of the severe risk over the area. In the \nmid-levels, a vort max will push east ahead of the trough axis \nproviding mid-level support at the same time a strong (35-45 knot) \nLLJ moves over the area. This coupled with dewpoints climbing into \nthe upper 60s to low 70s on Tuesday should allow for thunderstorms \ndevelopment, especially east of I-75 where there is the greatest \npotential for destabilization to occur by Tuesday afternoon. \n\nThe tricky part about the Tuesday forecast is a result of \nuncertainty regarding how the decaying overnight/early morning \nconvection will impact destabilization across the area. If cloud \ncover lingers longer than it is currently forecast to, then there \ncould be less instability that develops come the afternoon, limiting \nthe overall robustness of storms across the area and placing the \naxis of severe storms further to the southeast. On the flip side, if \nthe cloud coverage clears quickly and instability quickly increases, \nthe region could see a shift in the severe potential to include \nareas further west. \n\nOpted to ride the middle ground with this forecast package with some \ncloud coverage lingering, but instability still increasing to over \n2000 J/kg given the strong WAA and moisture expected. With all of \nthat, SPC has maintained the Day 2 Enhanced Risk (3 of 5) for the \nextreme far southeastern counties and a Slight Risk (2 of 5) for the \nbulk of the remainder of the area with the exception of the Toledo \nmetro area. With this setup, the favored storm mode would be a large \nMCC with the primary threat being damaging winds up to 70mph and \npotentially a few embedded tornadoes. There is a non-zero chance for \nisolated large hail. Will have to monitor overnight convection \ntrends to get a better grasp on the potential tomorrow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nBehind the robust cold front that moves east tomorrow, temperatures \nare expected to cool quite a bit, falling well below average for \nthis time of year on Wednesday and Thursday. Ahead of the boundary, \n850mb temperatures of 17-19C will push out of the region as 850mb \ntemperatures closer to 7-8C push south. This quick change in \ntemperatures will result in breezy conditions and a quick shift in \nsurface temperatures as well. Highs on Tuesday will climb into \nthe low to mid 80s, but will struggle to get out of the low to \nmid 70s through Thursday with overnight lows expected to drop \ninto the 50s. This setup may also result in lake enhanced \nshowers impacting portions of the snowbelt given the very warm \nlake temperatures. As high pressure returns late Thursday into \nFriday, temperatures will slowly climb back to near normal for \nthis weekend with highs expected to be in the 80s once again.",
